The Reckoning of Numbers.

Hamlet, the examiner.
Romeo, the answer.

Act I: Integer arithmetic.
Scene I: The reckoning.

[Enter Hamlet and Romeo]

Hamlet:
 You are as good as the square of the sum of a fair flower and a flower.
 Open your heart!
 You are as good as a fair fair fair fair fair flower.
 Speak your mind!
 You are as good as the square root of the sum of a fair fair fair flower and the sum of a fair fair flower and the sum of a fair flower and a flower.
 Open your heart!
 You are as good as a fair fair fair fair fair flower.
 Speak your mind!
 You are as good as the quotient between the difference between nothing and the sum of a fair fair flower and the sum of a fair flower and a flower and a fair flower.
 Open your heart!
 You are as good as a fair fair fair fair fair flower.
 Speak your mind!
 You are as good as the product of the difference between nothing and a fair flower and the difference between nothing and a fair flower.
 Open your heart!
 You are as good as a fair fair fair fair fair flower.
 Speak your mind!
 You are as good as the remainder of the quotient between the sum of a fair fair fair fair flower and a flower and the sum of a fair fair flower and a flower.
 Open your heart!
 You are as good as the sum of a fair fair fair flower and a fair flower.
 Speak your mind!

[Exeunt]
